window.DOCS_SEARCH_EN = [{"u":"index-en.html","t":"What is Zyllio?","x":"Describe your application. Zyllio builds it with you. AI generates your company’s business applications: screens, data and automations. You stay in control in the NoCode Studio.  Zyllio is an all-in-one comprehensive solution that allows you to create professional mobile applications, regardless of the field you are in. From the design of the data schema, setting up a unique design, creating dynamic interactions, and using different compatible platforms, to publication, Zyllio guides you with its intuitive interface, innovative features, and a team always ready to assist you. Want to master this tool ? Follow the guide!","s":[{"id":"overview","h":"Overview","x":"Zyllio offers a complete studio, composed of different \"rooms\" that will allow you to have a logical and smooth flow, from setting up a custom design to integrating external data or directly from the built-in database management."},{"id":"bug","h":"Found a bug?","x":"Zyllio is constantly evolving, and like everyone, we might overlook certain bugs. If you want to report a problem or malfunction, you can do so from your dashboard, in the Community section. You will help us constantly improve our products; your feedback is of great value to us! They will all be read!"},{"id":"contact","h":"Contact","x":"You can contact us on our social media and our Slack. Website LinkedIn Docs"}]},{"u":"en/actions.html","t":"Zyllio Studio Actions Hints","x":"This document provides a comprehensive list of all Zyllio actions with their descriptions (hints).","s":[{"id":"account","h":"Account","x":"Label Description Create application Creates a new application instance within the account workspace. Publish application Publishes the current application version, making it live and accessible to users. Sync application Synchronizes application state and configurations across workspace environments. Update application Updates application metadata, settings, and workspace preferences."},{"id":"authentication","h":"Authentication","x":"Label Description Change password Triggers a password change request for the currently logged-in user. Disconnect Logs out the current user, clearing session tokens and navigating to the sign-in screen. Login Authenticates a user with a username and password, starting a secure session. Reset password Sends a password reset email or code to help recovery for a user account. Show user profile Displays the user profile management panel to edit personal details and settings. Sign Up Registers a new user account with specified profile fields and credentials. SSO Disconnect Logs out a user from their Single Sign-On (SSO) session. SSO Login Initiates a Single Sign-On (SSO) login flow using configured identity providers."},{"id":"bluetooth","h":"Bluetooth","x":"Label Description Bluetooth connect Prompts the user to select and connect to a nearby Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) device. Bluetooth notification Subscribes to notifications or indications from a connected Bluetooth device characteristic. Bluetooth write Writes value data bytes to a specific service characteristic on a connected Bluetooth device."},{"id":"calendar","h":"Calendar","x":"Label Description Install event Installs and adds a custom event with details directly to the device calendar."},{"id":"controls","h":"Controls","x":"Label Description Continue if Halts workflow execution if a specified condition is not validated. Début boucle Starts a loop sequence to execute nested actions repeatedly over a list of items. Decision point Evaluates a condition during action execution to branch the workflow path. Fin boucle Marks the end boundary of a loop block in the workflow sequence."},{"id":"data","h":"Data","x":"Label Description Create a row Inserts a new record row into a database table with configured column values. Delete a row Deletes a specific row record from a database table matching a target identifier. Delete rows Deletes multiple row records from a database table matching query filters. Delete selected row Deletes the currently selected row in a list or table component. Export CSV Generates and triggers a download of a CSV file containing records from a table. Update a row Updates column values for a specific record row in a database table. Update selected row Updates column values for the currently selected row in a list or table component."},{"id":"device","h":"Device","x":"Label Description Get current position Requests device GPS sensors to fetch the user's current geographic coordinates. Voice recognition Starts voice recording and triggers speech-to-text recognition on the device."},{"id":"dialog","h":"Dialog","x":"Label Description Show confirmation Displays a confirmation dialog requesting the user to approve or cancel an action. Show message Displays a custom modal alert message box with customizable text and close buttons. Show notification Displays a temporary notification banner alert on the screen."},{"id":"messaging","h":"Messaging","x":"Label Description Send Email Sends an email message via Gmail with specified recipient, subject, and body. Send SMS Sends an SMS text message using Twilio gateway integrations. Send Whatsapp Sends a WhatsApp chat message using Twilio API configurations."},{"id":"navigation","h":"Navigation","x":"Label Description Close popup Closes the currently active popup screen window. Go back Navigates back to the previous screen in the navigation history. Go home Navigates directly to the designated home screen of the application. Go to screen Navigates directly to a target screen specified by name or identifier. Navigate website Redirects the browser or app to an external URL or web address. Open popup Opens a designated screen as a modal popup window. Refresh screen Reloads data and updates all components on the current active screen. Wait Pauses workflow action execution for a specified duration in seconds."},{"id":"offline","h":"Offline","x":"Label Description Offline sync Triggers synchronization of offline local database changes with the remote server."},{"id":"openai","h":"Openai","x":"Label Description Generate Image Uses OpenAI Dall-E model services to generate custom images based on text prompts. Generate Text Uses OpenAI GPT models to generate text responses from input prompts. Speech to text Transcribes audio voice recordings into text logs using OpenAI Whisper model. Text to speech Synthesizes spoken audio files from text copy using OpenAI voice models."},{"id":"payment","h":"Payment","x":"Label Description Add product Adds a product item and quantity to the active shopping cart selection. Cancel payment Cancels an ongoing subscription checkout payment flow. Confirm payment Confirms and finalizes a pending payment transaction. Create account Registers a user profile account with payment service providers. Customer portal Redirects users to the payment billing management portal. Process payment Initiates a checkout transaction flow for cart items. Process subscription Initiates a recurring billing subscription flow for a checkout plan. Remove product Removes product items or decreases their quantities in the shopping cart. Reset cart Clears all product items and resets the active shopping cart to empty."},{"id":"push-notification","h":"Push-notification","x":"Label Description Send to all Dispatches a push notification broadcast to all registered application users. Send to channel Dispatches a push notification message to users subscribed to a target channel. Send to user Dispatches a push notification message target to a specific user ID. Subscribe Subscribes the device terminal to push notification alerts."},{"id":"qrcode","h":"Qrcode","x":"Label Description Scan QR Code Opens the device camera interface to scan and decode a QR Code."},{"id":"rest","h":"Rest","x":"Label Description Delete Dispatches a DELETE request call to a specified REST API endpoint. Get Dispatches a GET request call to fetch resource payloads from a REST API endpoint. Patch Dispatches a PATCH request call to modify specific resource fields in a REST API endpoint. Post Dispatches a POST request call to submit payload data to a REST API endpoint. Put Dispatches a PUT request call to write or replace resource data in a REST API endpoint."},{"id":"theme","h":"Theme","x":"Label Description Zoom in text Increases the global theme typography size scale for text zoom in. Zoom out text Decreases the global theme typography size scale for text zoom out."},{"id":"variable","h":"Variable","x":"Label Description Clear selection Resets the currently selected row or item variable pointer to empty. Decrement Decreases the numeric value of a specified variable by 1. Increment Increases the numeric value of a specified variable by 1. Load variables Loads and initializes variables into the application session state. Reset variables Resets all session variables or a target variable back to their default values. Set selection variable Sets a selection variable pointer to reference a target row item. Set variable Assigns a specified static or dynamic value to a target variable. Set variable if Assigns a value to a variable only if a specified condition is validated."},{"id":"webhook","h":"Webhook","x":"Label Description Make Webhook Sends data payload triggers to a configured webhook endpoint. n8n Webhook Dispatches trigger data payloads specifically to an n8n workflow webhook endpoint. Zapier Webhook Dispatches trigger data payloads specifically to a Zapier webhook integration endpoint."}]},{"u":"en/ai-component-generation-92.html","t":"AI component generation","x":"AI component generation","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Examples","x":"Create a Cards component that displays a product catalog as a grid from a table. Consider the image, name, description, and price columns (numeric without currency). Each card is laid out vertically, with the image at the top, then the title, then the description, then an Add to cart button and a Favorites button Create an Accordion component that lists a table with the properties title (the accordion name), and in the open state content: subtitle, content, and image. Opening must be animated Create a modern responsive list for each item - left side: the image (Image column) - right side in responsive mode: the title, subtitle below, category (as a pill) in the corner. Ability to select. Show a chevron arrow to the right of each row (to the right of the category). Also add a hover color. And a click action create a diamond-shaped button with 2 colors create an analog clock component with the option to hide the numbers Create a static \"Tabs\" component with 2 tabs (title, an icon using UTF (not an image), content) Create a modern dynamic \"Dynamic tabs\" component from a table with the columns: title, image with rounded corners, content Create a Vertical list component with Title and s"}]},{"u":"en/airtable-13.html","t":"Airtable","x":"Zyllio vous permet de connecter facilement vos bases de données Airtable à une application mobile sans avoir besoin de connaissances en programmation. Grâce à cette intégration, vous pouvez accéder, visualiser et interagir avec vos données Airtable en temps réel, directement depuis votre application.  Zyllio Configuration Log in to Zyllio. Select the application to integrate with Airtable. Select the Settings tab, then Airtable. Enable the Airtable integration. Paste the token copied from the Airtable portal as follows: Importing Airtable Tables and Views From the Database tab, click on the Airtable option in the Import table section on the left. Then select the table(s) to import. This operation can be repeated to import additional tables later. Once confirmed, Zyllio imports all the selected tables along with their field types and relationships. For example, five imported tables with relationships. Calculated fields such as formulas, lookups, rollups, and IDs are indicated with a lock icon. These fields cannot be modified from Zyllio. Adjustments are sometimes necessary to provide Zyllio with more information about the field type. For example, the Image field in the Products tabl","s":[{"id":"configuration","h":"Configuration","x":"Airtable Configuration Before you start importing your Airtable tables and views, you need to set up the Airtable integration. Here are the steps to follow: Log in to your Airtable account. Access the integration settings from the Builder Hub section of your account. Then select the Personal access tokens option from the left menu. Click on the Create new token button. Enter a name, for example: Zyllio. Then select the following scopes: Validate the creation of the token. Once created, click the Copy button to copy the token. Once this dialog box is closed, Airtable will no longer display the token. You will need to generate another one to obtain it."},{"id":"structure-synchronization","h":"Structure Synchronization","x":"When you make structural changes in your Airtable tables, such as adding a field, these changes will not appear in Zyllio. To synchronize these changes, click the Refresh Structure button. Once the synchronization process is initiated, Zyllio will add and remove fields based on the changes. Zyllio does not modify the structure of Airtable tables in your Airtable account. Only the Zyllio definition can be modified from within Zyllio."},{"id":"data-synchronization","h":"Data Synchronization","x":"Zyllio implements a data cache to improve access performance to Airtable based on the usage of Airtable tables. As a result, changes made from Airtable or external automation may not be immediately visible in Zyllio. A delay of up to 1 minute may be necessary. To force a refresh, click the Refresh Data button."},{"id":"common-errors","h":"Common errors","x":""}]},{"u":"en/api-saas-87.html","t":"API SaaS","x":"API SaaS Available from the Enterprise plan","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Introduction","x":"This documentation describes the available APIs for setting up a SaaS application that lets you automate: Creating applications from another application template Publishing applications Updating applications Synchronizing applications The URL prefix is: https://www.zyllio.one/api/account/"},{"id":"endpoint-documentation","h":"Endpoint documentation","x":"Method Path Headers Body Comment Response GET /applications x-api-key - Lists the applications linked to the API key ApplicationSummaryModel[] POST /applications x-api-key CreateApplicationModel Creates an application from a template string PUT /sync-applications/:id x-api-key SyncApplicationModel Synchronizes an application void PUT /applications/:id x-api-key UpdateApplicationModel Updates an application void POST /publications/:id x-api-key - Publishes an application void"},{"id":"types","h":"Types","x":"ApplicationSummaryModel Field Type Description id string Unique application identifier name string Application name lastUpdated Date Last update date screenNumber number Number of screens tableNumber number Number of tables pluginNumber number Number of plugins userName string Owner name email string Owner email description string Application description url string Application URL shares string[] Emails or IDs of shares icon string Icon URL or path CreateApplicationModel Field Type Description templateId string Template ID to use for creation SyncApplicationModel Field Type Description fromApplicationId string Source application ID to synchronize screens boolean Synchronize screens ( true / false ) theme boolean Synchronize the theme ( true / false ) UpdateApplicationModel Field Type Description name string Application name description string Application description icon string Icon URL or path"},{"id":"create-a-saas-application","h":"Create a SaaS application","x":"API key The API key is available in your account settings from the main screen This section lets you copy the API key Define the REST APIs You need to define 2 configurations in the REST settings applications - to create and update applications publications - to publish applications then List applications Applications can be imported from the Data tab Click the Import menu, then REST, select Applications and finally Import A dynamic REST table contains the applications. You just need to use a List or Grid component to display the applications Create an application Suppose you want to provide a home screen that allows your users to create an application from one of your applications that serves as a template To create an application, you must use the REST Post action as shown below The templateId parameter refers to your template application. The result of this call is a new application identifier called Application Id Update an application To update an application, use a REST Put action The Additional Path parameter refers to the application created by the previous action Note that the formula contains a / before the variable The Request Data parameter defines the new application n"}]},{"u":"en/application-settings-91.html","t":"Application settings","x":"Application settings You can pass information to your Zyllio application directly through its URL. This makes it possible to control the display or pass data, such as a user name or an ID, as soon as the app opens.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"URL structure","x":"Parameters are added after your application ID, starting with a question mark ( ? ). Case A: Specify a specific screen (Optional) If you want the application to open on a specific screen rather than the default home screen, place the screen name in the first position. Example: https://zyllio.link/apps/<appId>/?SalesScreen Case B: Pass data without changing the screen If you only want to pass data without changing the start screen, list your parameters directly. Example: https://zyllio.link/apps/<appId>/?Name=Didier"},{"id":"combining-multiple-parameters","h":"Combining multiple parameters","x":"To combine the start screen and extra data, use the & symbol as a separator. Golden rule: If you specify a start screen, it must be the first parameter after the ? . Full example: https://zyllio.link/apps/d0f269524cd4/?Dashboard&Name=Didier&Status=Premium In this example: The application opens on the Dashboard screen. It passes the Name (Didier) and Status (Premium) variables."},{"id":"how-do-you-retrieve-these-values","h":"How do you retrieve these values?","x":"To use this data in your no-code logic, for example to display a welcome message or filter a list: Go to the Application section in the editor. Choose the \"Application parameter\" function. Enter the name of the key used in the URL (for example: Name )."},{"id":"handling-special-characters-encoding","h":"Handling special characters (encoding)","x":"URLs cannot contain spaces or accented characters directly. If your screen names or parameter values include them, they must be encoded. Basic rules Spaces: Replace them with %20 . Accents: It is strongly recommended to avoid them in your screen names. If you need to pass a value with an accent (for example, \"Sebastien\"), the URL will often transform it automatically. Conversion examples Desired text URL format Dashboard screen ?Dashboard%20screen First name = Heloise ?FirstName=H%C3%A9lo%C3%AFse"}]},{"u":"en/authentication-61.html","t":"Authentication","x":"Authentication secures access and personalizes the user experience.","s":[{"id":"authentication","h":"Authentication","x":"Authentication protects user data from unauthorized access. By verifying the user's identity before granting access to their account or sensitive data, the application ensures that only authorized individuals can view or modify this information. With authentication, an application can provide a personalized experience by remembering preferences, purchase histories, account settings, etc. This allows for a more tailored and enjoyable service for the user. Authentication helps manage access levels within the application. By identifying the user, the application can determine which features or data are accessible to them based on their role or permissions."},{"id":"defining-a-users-table","h":"Defining a Users Table","x":"Users are defined in a table of your choice, which must meet the following prerequisites: It must contain a column called Name. It must contain a column called Profile. It must contain a column of type Email called Email. It must contain a column of type Password called Password. Other columns characterizing users, which are not necessary for authentication, can be defined, such as their date of birth, city, or role. The Table Templates section allows you to define a ready-to-use user table. Simply click and drag the Users template into the workspace. The following Users table is created: This table is a starting point and can be modified according to needs as long as the above prerequisites are met. The users table can also be defined in an external data source such as Google Sheets, TimeTonic, or Airtable."},{"id":"user-management","h":"User Management","x":"User management involves editing the data in the users table either: From Zyllio, in the Database tab, using the Edit button on the table. From Airtable, TimeTonic, or Google Sheets if the table is external. Users can be modified from this dialog, including adding and deleting users. Additionally, a users table can be imported via a CSV file."},{"id":"authenticating-a-user","h":"Authenticating a User","x":"Authentication Screen Authentication is triggered from a screen where a form is defined with at least two input fields: Email. Password. This screen can be created from scratch or using the pre-built screen template called Login. Authentication Button A button is necessary to trigger authentication. This button must define an action that calls the Login action available from the action editor in the Authentication section. This action defines the Login action parameters. Redirection After Authentication Two approaches are possible to redirect the user after authentication: The first approach is to define a transition from the button that triggered the authentication. The transition will occur only if the authentication is successful. The second approach is to add a Go To Screen action, which will be triggered only if the authentication is successful. In general, an action that fails interrupts the chain of actions: the next action is not executed."},{"id":"authenticated-user-variable","h":"Authenticated User Variable","x":"Once the user is successfully authenticated, a variable called User (default name) is made available for further use. For example: Display the name or email of the authenticated user. Associate the user with a new record, such as the user's email in an Orders table or a Favorites table. Use the email for sending an email or Push Notifications. ... This variable is accessible from the Variables section, then the Authentication Screen, and then User."},{"id":"logging-out-a-user","h":"Logging Out a User","x":"The user can log out, and the user profile panel is accessible from the header of the screens."}]},{"u":"en/chat-67.html","t":"Chat","x":"This guide explains how to set up and configure a Chat component that enables message exchange between 2 authenticated users.","s":[{"id":"setting-up-authentication","h":"Setting Up Authentication","x":"Before you begin, it is essential to set up authentication to ensure that only authorized users can exchange messages with other users. Creating the Users Table From the Database tab, drag a Users table into the workspace. Zyllio will automatically create the required columns and also 2 sample users. These users are useful for testing and can later be modified or deleted. You can keep tables that define columns in English. However, screens can still be displayed in your language by using the Label property available in all components. Creating the Authentication Screen From the Designer tab, drag a Login screen into the workspace while selecting the Users table. You get this ready-to-use screen: Then a second Header screen that we will name Friends:"},{"id":"displaying-the-friends-list","h":"Displaying the Friends List","x":"Connect the Enter button, which handles authentication, to the Friends screen. That way, if authentication succeeds, the mobile app will display the linked screen (here Friends): Add a List component in the Friends screen (or any other list component such as Grid or Carousel) using the Users table: Define a filter on this List component to exclude the authenticated user using this criterion: Email is not equal to User/Email . Email refers to the Email column of the Users table and User/Email is the email of the authenticated user (User):"},{"id":"creating-the-my-messages-screen","h":"Creating the \"My Messages\" Screen","x":"Creating the Chat messages Table You need to create a table to store messages written by users. To do this, from the Database tab, drag the Chat messages table into the workspace: Creating the Chat Component Create a screen named My messages and drag a Chat component from the components list in the Lists section, selecting the Chat messages table. Then link the 2 screens as below: The Chat component shows no message because the table is empty. Filtering Messages By default, the Chat component shows all messages sent and received by the authenticated user. Here the Chat component should display only the messages exchanged with the user selected in the Friends screen. To do this, set the Filter by recipient email property to the value Friends/Email, which is the selected friend in the Friends screen."},{"id":"writing-messages","h":"Writing Messages","x":"Optionally, to allow message writing you need to use the Message component in the Form fields section, again selecting the Chat messages table. In this tutorial, the authenticated user sends a message to the friend selected in the Friends screen. You must therefore fill in the Recipient email property:"}]},{"u":"en/components.html","t":"Zyllio Studio Components Hints","x":"This document provides a comprehensive list of all Zyllio components with their descriptions (hints).","s":[{"id":"basics","h":"Basics","x":"Label Description Bulleted list A list component that displays items as a bulleted or numbered sequence. It supports icon customization and custom list formatting options. Perfect for displaying features, benefits, or sequential steps. Header A top navigation header bar that displays a page title along with optional actions like back buttons or custom action buttons. It supports custom alignment, gradients, borders, and typography. Used to provide structure and navigation consistency at the top of screens. Icon A component that displays a single customizable icon from standard icon packs alongside optional text. It supports custom sizes, colors, background fills, and borders to serve as visual indicators or interactive buttons. Image A versatile component designed to display images with options for different layouts, profile mode (circular cropping), labels, and descriptions. It supports click actions, custom borders, padding, and alignments. Essential for displaying visual content, user avatars, or media banners. Label button A contact button that displays an icon, text, and an action trigger. It is configured to initiate direct interactions such as dialing a phone number, sending"},{"id":"buttons","h":"Buttons","x":"Label Description Button bar A horizontal group of multiple buttons. Used to present a set of related actions aligned next to each other. Favorite A toggle button for favoriting items in a list or database. Automatically updates the relation between the user and the item. Float A floating action button that hovers on top of the screen contents. Often placed in the bottom right corner for primary actions. Follow A toggle button to follow or unfollow items, creators, or topics. Manages follow states and triggers customizable transition actions. Icon A small circular button displaying only an icon. Designed for compact actions or icon-only triggers. Like A toggle button to like or unlike content. Connects directly to a database table to keep track of user likes. Link A button styled as a simple text hyperlink. Ideal for secondary actions, terms of service, or clean layouts. Normal A standard solid action button. Designed for primary call-to-actions with customizable colors, labels, and click events. Outline A button with a transparent background and a colored border outline. Commonly used for secondary or less prominent actions."},{"id":"contacts","h":"Contacts","x":"Label Description Download file A button configured to download a specified file. Useful for sharing PDF documents, assets, or resources. Email A contact button that launches the default email client with a pre-filled recipient address. Ideal for direct support queries. Export data A component that allows users to export data from tables or databases. Supports format configurations for downloading files. Facebook page A button that redirects users to a specified Facebook profile or page. Helps increase social media presence. Instagram A button that opens a specified Instagram profile. Perfect for linking to visual portfolios or social accounts. Link A button that opens an external website link in a new browser tab. Perfect for linking to resources or external pages. Phone A button that opens the device dialer with a pre-filled phone number. Allows users to start a phone call instantly. Profile A profile information block displaying a user avatar, username, and metadata details. Ideal for profile cards or user directories. QR Code VCard A component that generates and displays a QR Code containing contact details. Users can scan it to instantly save contact information to their phone"},{"id":"container","h":"Container","x":"Label Description Container A layout container component used to group and arrange child elements. It supports flex configurations, backgrounds, padding, and borders."},{"id":"dashboard","h":"Dashboard","x":"Label Description Chart A visual data representation component supporting line, bar, pie, and doughnut charts. Connects directly to database tables to visualize values dynamically. Progress A visual progress bar that displays completion percentages or ratios. Ideal for tracking tasks, goals, or loading states. Statistic A dashboard card designed to highlight key metrics, counts, or summary values. Supports big labels and descriptive metadata text."},{"id":"form-fields","h":"Form-fields","x":"Label Description Capture audio An audio recorder component allowing users to capture sound directly from their device microphone. Useful for voice notes. Checkbox A standard checkbox input field for binary (yes/no) choices. Supports custom labels, default values, and change events. Choose options An input component displaying options as selectable chips or pills. Allows users to pick single or multiple options easily. Comment A text area input field designed specifically for writing comments. Includes placeholder options and action triggers. Date A date input field with a built-in calendar picker. Helps users select dates accurately with custom format configurations. Dropdown A selection dropdown menu input field. Ideal for selecting a single item from a long list of options. Email An email input field optimized for email addresses. Includes basic syntax validation and virtual keyboard type support. File A file uploader component allowing users to select and upload documents or assets. Supports file size limits and format constraints. Image An image uploader component supporting drag-and-drop or device camera capture. Ideal for profile pictures or attachment uploads. Message A tex"},{"id":"geolocation","h":"Geolocation","x":"Label Description Map An interactive map component that displays geographical data and user locations. Supports custom layers and zoom configurations. Marker A map pin/marker component used to pinpoint specific locations on a map layout. Supports popup descriptions and click actions."},{"id":"lists","h":"Lists","x":"Label Description Calendar A calendar grid component that displays events, tasks, or schedules. Supports daily, weekly, or monthly layouts linked to data sources. Card A content card list item displaying titles, descriptions, and media. Perfect for list items, news previews, or product listings. Carousel A horizontal sliding container displaying a list of elements sequentially. Supports auto-play, navigation arrows, and paginators. Categories A category navigation list displaying items as circular icons or tags. Useful for filtering content or navigating catalog sections. Chat A dynamic chat thread component that displays messages with distinct sender and receiver styles. Connects to database tables for real-time messages. Comments A discussion list component designed to display user comments. Supports avatars, timestamps, and replying triggers. Gallery An image gallery layout component displaying photos in grid grids. Ideal for visual portfolios, albums, or media libraries. Grid A flexible grid list layout component displaying database items in columns and rows. Highly customizable layout style. List A standard vertical list layout component designed to display database items sequ"},{"id":"media","h":"Media","x":"Label Description Audio An audio player component that plays audio tracks or streaming content. Supports volume, progress controls, and play/pause buttons. Embed An iframe embedding component used to display external web content directly inside a page. Ideal for external forms or calculators. QR Code A component that dynamically generates and displays QR codes from text, URLs, or variable values. Perfect for sharing links. Video A native video player component that streams or plays video files. Supports standard media playback controls. Youtube video A component designed to embed YouTube videos cleanly. Supports standard playback parameters and player configurations."},{"id":"payment","h":"Payment","x":"Label Description Cart A checkout cart component showing items, quantities, and price summaries. Essential for online shopping and order submission workflows."},{"id":"tables","h":"Tables","x":"Label Description Table A standard data table component used to organize and display relational data in columns and rows. Supports column styling and sorting. Table editor A tabular data grid editor allowing users to directly insert, update, or delete rows. Ideal for backoffice management."},{"id":"website","h":"Website","x":"Label Description Banner A hero banner header component for website pages. Supports background images, titles, action buttons, and promotional messages."}]},{"u":"en/custom-domain-53.html","t":"Custom domain","x":"Publish your Zyllio application using your own domain name, such as www.my-app.com.  Available with the Professional plan. This integration lets you associate a domain name with your Zyllio application, then activate it with an SSL certificate.","s":[{"id":"enable-the-custom-domain","h":"Enable the custom domain","x":"In Zyllio, open your application settings, then the Custom domain section. Enable the integration using the toggle at the top of the page."},{"id":"1-enter-your-domain","h":"1. Enter your domain","x":"In the Domain name field, enter the address you want to use, for example www.my-app.com. You can enter the domain with or without https://. Zyllio automatically removes the protocol and any path so that only the domain name is retained. The domain must be valid and include at least one subdomain and a top-level domain. Make sure you have purchased it and can modify its DNS records through your domain provider, such as OVH, Gandi, or Namecheap."},{"id":"2-generate-the-dns-configuration","h":"2. Generate the DNS configuration","x":"Click Configure. Zyllio immediately generates the DNS settings required for your domain. The page displays a single DNS record with its type, host, and value. Depending on your domain, this is either an A or CNAME record."},{"id":"3-add-the-dns-record","h":"3. Add the DNS record","x":"Sign in to your domain provider's administration interface, then open its DNS record management page. Add the record displayed by Zyllio, using its exact type, host, and value. Do not replace these values with a generic configuration."},{"id":"4-check-dns-propagation","h":"4. Check DNS propagation","x":"After saving the change with your provider, return to Zyllio and click Check propagation. DNS propagation can take from 5 minutes to 24 hours. If the check fails, wait a few minutes and try again. Also check that the type, host, and value of the record exactly match those displayed in Zyllio."},{"id":"5-activate-the-secure-domain","h":"5. Activate the secure domain","x":"When the check succeeds, click Activate secure domain. Zyllio then generates the SSL certificate and deploys your application on your domain over HTTPS. Once activation is complete, your users can access the application at the configured address."}]},{"u":"en/dashboard-7.html","t":"Dashboard","x":"The dashboard serves as the central control point for managing your application creation space. This page provides you with quick access to essential features, making it easier to manage your applications and navigate through the different aspects of the platform.","s":[{"id":"application-management","h":"Application Management","x":"The \"Applications\" section allows you to create, delete, clone, and edit your applications. Available actions: Create a new application Delete an existing application Clone an application to save time in development or to make a backup Edit the settings and features of each application"},{"id":"application-templates","h":"Application Templates","x":"Discover application templates that can serve as a starting point for creating your own applications. These templates are business examples inspired by real needs. Features: Variety of templates suited for different sectors Starting point to accelerate the development process Customizable according to specific needs"},{"id":"access-to-published-applications","h":"Access to Published Applications","x":"View and manage published applications. Features: Complete list of published applications and their statuses Publication dates Direct link to the applications"},{"id":"access-to-shared-applications","h":"Access to Shared Applications","x":"Explore shared applications to collaborate with other creators and extend the features of your own projects. Possibilities: Access applications shared by other users Collaborate on common projects Share your applications to encourage collaboration"},{"id":"documentation","h":"Documentation","x":"Find detailed information on every aspect of the platform. Comprehensive documentation is available to guide you at every step. Included sections: Quick start guide In-depth tutorials Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)"},{"id":"analytics","h":"Analytics","x":"Explore analytical data to understand user behavior, app performance, and make informed decisions. Track usage statistics and ensure that your users always enjoy the best experience. Offered analyses: Track usage and installation of your applications Monitor active users Evaluate feature performance"},{"id":"forums","h":"Forums","x":"Participate in discussions, ask questions, and share your experiences with the user community. Forum features: Thematic sections for targeted discussions Mutual assistance between users Direct interaction with the support team"},{"id":"tutorials","h":"Tutorials","x":"Explore a library of tutorials to master every feature of the software. Visual resources will guide you through important steps. Tutorial categories: Application creation Advanced customization Data management Integration with third-party systems User authentication"},{"id":"blog","h":"Blog","x":"Stay informed about the latest updates, development tips, and inspiring community stories. Blog content: Announcements of new features Tips and best practices User success stories"}]},{"u":"en/design-tool-4.html","t":"Design tool","x":"At the top of the editor portal, a navigation bar gives access to the main work areas of the application.","s":[{"id":"main-tabs","h":"Main tabs","x":"The top bar organizes the work into 6 complementary spaces: Screens to build, Transitions to connect, Scenarios to automate, Simulation to test, Data to model, and Settings to configure."},{"id":"screens","h":"Screens","x":"Main entry point for designing the application interface. This tab is used to create and organize screens, position visual components, and define the user experience."},{"id":"transitions","h":"Transitions","x":"Used to manage navigation between screens. It defines links, sequences, and visual transitions that structure the user journey."},{"id":"scenarios","h":"Scenarios","x":"Dedicated space for workflows and automation. It is used to create, edit, and manage scenarios triggered by a user action, a webhook, a schedule, or a data event. Learn more: Scenarios ."},{"id":"simulation","h":"Simulation","x":"Used to test the application in conditions close to real usage. You can choose a user, change the device type, and restart the simulation to verify the application's behavior."},{"id":"data","h":"Data","x":"Area for managing the data model. This tab allows you to work with the application's database, its tables, columns, and relationships."},{"id":"settings","h":"Settings","x":"Centralizes the application's general configuration. It includes application information, plugins, security, and integrations organized by category."},{"id":"top-right-actions","h":"Top-right actions","x":"Publish Triggers publication of the application so it becomes available in its deployable version. Open Displays access to the published application, including the available open and sharing options."}]},{"u":"en/faq-86.html","t":"FAQ","x":"FAQ","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Which QR code formats are supported","x":""},{"id":"which-date-format-is-supported-by-zyllio","h":"Which date format is supported by Zyllio","x":"Zyllio natively supports the ISO 8601 format: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"},{"id":"common-airtable-errors","h":"Common Airtable errors","x":"Message Comment Insuficient permission to create new select option Airtable indicates that an Options-type column received a value that is not allowed. This is not related to your Personal Token. Please check the Options fields in your Zyllio action or your Airtable columns."},{"id":"common-timetonic-errors","h":"Common TimeTonic errors","x":"Message Comment Your data cannot be modified for technical reasons Check that you do not have a unique Attachment column. Attachments must be multiple."}]},{"u":"en/favoris-follower-like-74.html","t":"Favoris follower like","x":"The Favorites / Follower / Like features allow users to save or like specific content.  They play a key role in enhancing the user experience by enabling personalized interaction and facilitating quick access to important items. The Favorites, Followers, and Like features in Zyllio have similarities. This document uses Favorites as the main example, but the principles described below are also applicable to the other features.","s":[{"id":"database-configuration","h":"Database configuration","x":"To store the Favorites functionality, you need to use a predefined table called Favorites. From the Database tab, move a Favorites table into the workspace. The table must contain at least the following columns: User: This column stores the email address of the user adding a favorite. By default, it is often linked to User/Email. Favorite Item: This column represents the object or item that is marked as a favorite. For example: Products/Id for a product. This column must reference an item uniquely. Example structure of the Favorites table: User Favorite Item user1@email.com Product 45887 user2@email.com Product 11341"},{"id":"set-up-authentication","h":"Set up authentication","x":"Authentication allows each user to be identified in order to store their Favorites in a distinct and organized data space (for example, via their email). Favorites must be associated with a particular user to ensure they are accessible only to them. Create a users table In the Database tab, drag a Users table as follows: Note that this Users table provides 2 example users useful during the design phase of your application. Create an authentication screen Select the Login screen template and drag it into the workspace as follows:"},{"id":"add-an-items-component","h":"Add an items component","x":"Items are information to be favorited. Here we use a catalog of fruits and vegetables. From the Database tab, drag a Products table as follows: Note that this Products table provides 7 example products useful during the design phase of your application. Display items to favorite Use the Grid component, for example, to display the products on a new screen and link it to the authentication screen. Add a detail screen as follows. Add the favorite component Select the Favorite component, then the Favorites table, and drag the component into the detail screen as follows: Configuration of the favorites component The component requires configuration to specify the user who owns the Favorite as well as the item to be favorited. Favorites Component Parameter Description Example User User who owns the favorite Often the authenticated user User/Email Item Item to mark as favorite An item selected by the user Products/Name"},{"id":"test-favoriting","h":"Test favoriting","x":"Run the simulation and test by selecting one of the users. Finally, click on favorite. Note that the component automatically adds and removes Favorites."},{"id":"adding-to-favorites-without-authentication","h":"Adding to favorites without authentication","x":"It is possible to add items to Favorites without authenticating the user. To do this, in the settings of the Favorites component, use the terminal identifier via the formula Terminal / Identifier. You get: Not authenticating the user means that Favorites will only be displayed on the terminal where they were selected."}]},{"u":"en/first-application-50.html","t":"First application","x":"Create an Account","s":[{"id":"create-an-account","h":"Create an Account","x":"You can create a free account on Zyllio to get started. Each plan offers a range of different features. If you want to start with mobile app design on Zyllio, the free plan is an excellent choice and already offers a multitude of options. You will also be able to choose where your data is hosted. Do you want your applications to be hosted in France? No problem! Creating an account takes just a few seconds. Visit the homepage of the Zyllio website: https://zyllio.com/ Now click on the orange button \"Start for free.\" The next page allows you to choose where you want your account and applications to be hosted; in this case, we choose France. You then have the option to sign in with a Twitter or Google account. That's it, you should arrive at your Dashboard ! In the next section, we will set the stage to start our first application!"}]},{"u":"en/formulas-15.html","t":"Formulas","x":"Formulas The formula editor is a powerful tool within our No-Code platform that allows you to create custom formulas. These formulas can integrate text, functions, and enable the display of dynamic data such as variables, calculations, table data, images, and more.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Accessing the Editor","x":"Certain component and action properties support formulas, typically when the property expects text. For example, the Text property of the Label Text component. Click on the menu to the right of the property to view all available options. Here, the Text property can be populated from: A fixed text (without a variable) A formula A variable Select Formula to access the formula editor."},{"id":"formula-editor","h":"Formula Editor","x":"The formula editor allows you to combine text with contextual data. To do this, simply enter the text and drag and drop the required functions. For example: \"You have ordered [Count rows] products.\" Here, [Count rows] represents a dynamic data point (the result of a function) that calculates the number of rows from a table."},{"id":"functions","h":"Functions","x":"Category Description Condition Allows displaying text if a condition is true IF THEN IF THEN_ELSE Number Displays, generates, and calculates a number AMOUNT CALCULATE RANDOM Date Allows displaying a simple date, calculating a duration between two dates, and performing date calculations Current date & time Duration from today Add Substract Text Allows transforming a text, searching for, and replacing a pattern in a text Text Lowercase Uppercase Capitalize Concatenate Substring Replace Split Data Displays the result of an aggregation on tables Count rows Maximum in rows Minimum in rows Average in rows Sum in rows Image Displays an image. The path must be complete (absolute) Image Geolocation Displays the distance between the GPS position or between two positions Distance from position Distance Payment Displays a cart and details Print Cart Cart amount JSON Allows searching for information in a JSON message JSON Query"},{"id":"formats","h":"Formats","x":"Format Description Text Formats the data as text Please scan your barcode Whole number Formats the data as an integer. Decimal numbers are truncated (ignored). 2.2 => 2 077 => 77 123.45 => 123 Number Formats the data as a decimal number. The format supports a specific number of decimals and a unit. If the specified symbol is a dollar ($), the dollar symbol appears before the number. 1.2 => 1.20 € 12.23567 => 12 km 34.3654 => 4452.3 € 12.32 => $12.32 Currency Formats the data as an amount with currency. The number is displayed with 2 decimal places. 0.2 => 0.20€ 5.0 => $5.00 Distance Formats the data as a distance The most appropriate unit is used automatically. 5.2 => 5.2 m 85200 => 85.2 km Duration Formats the data as a duration The most appropriate unit is used automatically or can be manually selected. 5.2 => 5.2 s 600 => 5 m Rich text Displays HTML code <i>Italic</i> => i"}]},{"u":"en/functions.html","t":"Zyllio Studio Functions Hints","x":"This document provides a comprehensive list of all Zyllio functions with their descriptions (hints).","s":[{"id":"condition","h":"Condition","x":"Label Description If then A simple conditional block that returns a specified value only if the defined condition is met. If then else An conditional logic block that returns one value if a condition is true, and another if it is false."},{"id":"data","h":"Data","x":"Label Description Average in rows Calculates the average numeric value of a specified column across a set of table rows. Count rows Returns the total number of rows in a specified list or table selection. Count unique in rows Counts the number of distinct, unique values in a specified column across table rows. Maximum in rows Finds the highest numeric value in a specified column across a set of rows. Maximum text in rows Finds the maximum text value (alphabetically last) in a specified column across table rows. Minimum in rows Finds the lowest numeric value in a specified column across a set of rows. Minimum text in rows Finds the minimum text value (alphabetically first) in a specified column across table rows. Rows to text Concatenates and formats values from a specified column across multiple rows into a single text block. Search rows Filters and searches table rows matching specified query criteria. Sum in rows Calculates the sum total of all numeric values in a specified column across a set of rows."},{"id":"date","h":"Date","x":"Label Description Add Adds a specified amount of time (days, hours, minutes) to a base date. Current date & time Returns the current date and time of the system or device. Date & time Constructs or formats a custom date and time value from specified inputs. Duration from date range Calculates the time duration or difference between two specified date values. Duration from today Calculates the duration or time elapsed between a specified date and the current day. Substract Subtracts a specified amount of time (days, hours, minutes) from a base date."},{"id":"geolocation","h":"Geolocation","x":"Label Description Distance Calculates the straight-line distance between two specified geographical coordinates. Distance from position Calculates the geographical distance between a set coordinate and the user's current position."},{"id":"image","h":"Image","x":"Label Description Image Processes or resolves image source URLs and options for rendering."},{"id":"json","h":"Json","x":"Label Description JSON Query Queries and extracts specific values from a structured JSON payload using a search expression."},{"id":"list-item","h":"List-item","x":"Label Description Column Retrieves the value of a specific column from a list item or database row. List item index Returns the 0-based index position of an item within a list or selection. List item position Returns the 1-based display position of an item within its containing list."},{"id":"number","h":"Number","x":"Label Description Amount Formats a numeric value as a currency string with specified symbol and decimal settings. Calculation Evaluates a mathematical expression combining numbers, variables, and standard operators. Number Converts or formats inputs into a standardized numeric format. Random number Generates a pseudo-random numeric value within a specified minimum and maximum range."},{"id":"payment","h":"Payment","x":"Label Description Cart amount Calculates the total monetary amount of all items currently in the shopping cart. Cart product count Returns the total number of unique products currently added to the shopping cart. Cart quantity Returns the total aggregate quantity of all items in the shopping cart. Print cart Generates a formatted text representation of the current shopping cart contents for printing or receipts."},{"id":"saas","h":"Saas","x":"Label Description Application Id Retrieves the unique identifier of the running application instance. Application parameter Retrieves the value of a specific global application configuration parameter. Application Template Id Retrieves the template identifier associated with the running application. Table Id Retrieves the unique database identifier for a specified table name."},{"id":"string","h":"String","x":"Label Description Capitalize Converts the first letter of the text input to uppercase and the rest to lowercase. Concatenate Joins multiple text segments or variable values together into a single continuous string. Include Checks if a specified search term exists within a target text string. Join Concatenates elements of a list into a single string using a specified separator character. Length Returns the number of characters in a specified text string. Lower case Converts all characters in the text input to lowercase. Matche expression Checks if a text string matches a specified regular expression pattern. Occurences Counts how many times a search term appears within a target text string. Repeat text Repeats a specified text string a set number of times. Replace Replaces occurrences of a specified search string with a replacement string. Replace expression Replaces substrings matching a regular expression pattern with a replacement string. Split Splits a text string into an array of substrings based on a specified separator character. Substring Extracts a portion of a text string starting at a specified index with a set length. Text Converts a value or variable into a standardized"},{"id":"terminal","h":"Terminal","x":"Label Description Terminal Id Retrieves the unique identifier of the user's terminal or device. Terminal language Retrieves the primary language setting of the user's terminal or browser. Terminal OS Retrieves the operating system name of the user's terminal or device. Terminal type Retrieves the device category or type (e.g. desktop, mobile, tablet) of the terminal."},{"id":"translation","h":"Translation","x":"Label Description Translate Translates a key or label into the current active user interface language."}]},{"u":"en/generation-pdf-95.html","t":"PDF Generation","x":"Generate custom PDF documents like invoices, quotes, contracts, or certificates directly from your Zyllio application using Microsoft Word templates.  Available starting from the Enterprise plan. Zyllio's Documents integration allows you to dynamically populate Microsoft Word ( .docx ) templates with variables from your application and compile them into high-quality PDF files instantly, with no coding required.","s":[{"id":"1-enable-the-documents-integration","h":"1. Enable the Documents Integration","x":"Before generating any documents, you need to turn on the integration in your application settings: Open your application in Zyllio Studio . Navigate to the Settings tab in the main sidebar. Click on Integrations . Find the Documents card and toggle the Enable switch to the active position."},{"id":"2-prepare-your-word-template-docx","h":"2. Prepare your Word Template (.docx)","x":"You can design your template using Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice. Zyllio uses a simple expression language to map fields: Simple Fields To print simple text, numbers, or dates (like a client name or invoice date), insert the field name enclosed in double curly braces: Example: {{Client Name}} or {{Date}} Tip: Zyllio automatically normalizes your field names. Spaces, accents, and casing are ignored. For example, {{Nom Client}} and {{nomclient}} will both map to the same field. Tables & Lists (Loops) If you need to print a list of items (like invoice rows), you can create a loop. This tells Zyllio to repeat a section or a table row for every item in your list: Start the loop with: {{#items}} Define the fields for each item: {{description}} , {{quantity}} , {{price}} End the loop with: {{/items}} In a Word Table: Place the opening tag {{#items}} in the first cell of the row, and the closing tag {{/items}} in the last cell of the same row. Zyllio will automatically duplicate the row for each item. Example in a Word table: Description Quantity Price {{#items}}{{description}} {{quantity}} {{price}}{{/items}} Filtering Items You can filter list items directly inside the temp"},{"id":"3-upload-your-template-to-a-database-table","h":"3. Upload your Template to a Database Table","x":"Since the Zyllio Assets manager is reserved exclusively for images, you must store your Microsoft Word templates ( .docx ) in a database table: In your application database, create a table (for example, named Templates ). Add a column of type File to this table. Upload your .docx file into a row of this table."},{"id":"4-configure-the-generate-pdf-action","h":"4. Configure the \"Generate PDF\" Action","x":"Now, set up the action that will trigger the document generation (for example, on a button click): Select your button or trigger element on the screen. Open the Actions properties. Add a new step and select Generate PDF (under the Documents category). Configure the action properties: DOCX template URL: Bind this dynamically to the File column of the database record you uploaded in Step 3 (e.g., Templates.File ). Zyllio will automatically resolve it to the absolute file URL. Data fields: Map the variable tags from your Word document to your Zyllio variables or database collections. Open the editor to configure fields using two dedicated buttons: + Field: Adds a single value mapping (text, number, date, etc.). Specify the variable name in your Word template as the key (e.g., clientname ) and select a Zyllio screen variable or formula as the value. + List: Adds a list or table mapping. Specify the list/loop name in your Word template as the key (e.g., items ) and bind it to a database table or collection. PDF URL: Select or create a text variable (e.g., InvoicePdfUrl ) where the URL of the generated PDF will be stored. Loader: Set to True to show a loading spinner on the user's screen"},{"id":"5-display-download-or-store-the-generated-pdf","h":"5. Display, Download, or Store the Generated PDF","x":"Once Zyllio has generated the PDF and saved its URL into your variable, you can use it in three different ways in your application: A. Display the PDF To render the document directly inside your application screen for review: Add a PDF Viewer component to your screen. Bind its PDF URL property to the variable where you stored the generated URL (e.g., InvoicePdfUrl ). B. Download or Send the PDF To export or distribute the document: Attach the PDF URL variable to a download button or an \"open URL in browser\" action. Send it as an email attachment using Zyllio's email send actions. Forward the URL to external services using Zapier or Make actions. C. Store the PDF in the Database To archive the generated file for history or reference: Add a Create Row or Update Row action in the flow immediately following the PDF generation step. Select the target database table (e.g., InvoicesHistory ). Bind the table's File or URL column to the variable containing the generated PDF URL (e.g., InvoicePdfUrl ). The file will be saved permanently."}]},{"u":"en/google-sheets-11.html","t":"Google Sheets","x":"Google Sheets Zyllio lets you connect and use data from Google Sheets. With this feature, you can retrieve and use spreadsheets directly in your application.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Connecting to Google Sheets","x":"Open Zyllio Studio, go to the Settings tab in the left menu, select the Google Sheets option, and enable the Enable button. Then click the Connect button. This opens a window where you can select your Google account. Choose the Google account you want to use to access your Google Sheets. Once connected, you will see a status message indicating that the connection was successful: Connected."},{"id":"accessing-google-sheets","h":"Accessing Google Sheets","x":"From the Database tab, click the Import button to display external data sources, then select Sheets. The next window lets you select the Google Sheets document and then the sheet or sheets you want to import. Once imported, you get a new table."},{"id":"key-points","h":"Key Points","x":"Google Sheets does not include a mechanism to uniquely identify rows. To perform update operations, the first column must define a unique identifier. If this is not the case, a warning will be displayed. The first column must uniquely identify each row. The value can be a commonly used unique number, or any text, as long as uniqueness is respected. Example:"},{"id":"formats","h":"Formats","x":"The date format must be YYYY-MM-DD. Decimal numbers must use the Text format in the Sheet column and use a decimal point. Google Sheets columns with formulas should not be used. They do not cause any issue when reading data, but they may be overwritten during updates (Update-Row)."},{"id":"limitations","h":"Limitations","x":"Zyllio limits the Google Sheets range to 52 columns (A-Z and AA-AZ)."},{"id":"structure-synchronization","h":"Structure Synchronization","x":"When you make structural changes in your Google Sheets, such as adding columns, these changes do not appear in Zyllio. To synchronize these changes, click the Refresh Structure button. Once the synchronization process starts, Zyllio adds and removes fields based on the changes. Zyllio does not modify the column structure in your Google Sheets."},{"id":"data-synchronization","h":"Data Synchronization","x":"Zyllio sets up a data cache to improve Google Sheets access performance. As a result, changes made from Google Sheets or from an external automation may not be visible immediately in Zyllio. A delay of up to 1 minute may be required. To force a refresh, click the Refresh Data button."}]},{"u":"en/make-70.html","t":"Make","x":"Make is an online automation tool that connects your applications and services to automate repetitive tasks without the need for coding.  Available from the Enterprise plan. It works by linking two or more applications to create automations, called Scenarios, which trigger an action in one application when something happens in another. Zyllio offers a native integration with Make that allows: Triggering scenarios from Zyllio (via Webhook), Updating Zyllio data from Make scenarios.","s":[{"id":"prerequisites","h":"Prerequisites","x":"A valid Make account is required to connect the Make and Zyllio solutions. Additionally, a subscription to the Zyllio Enterprise plan is needed to activate this feature."},{"id":"configuration","h":"Configuration","x":"The Make integration can be activated from the Settings tab / Integration / Make by enabling the Enable button as shown in this image."},{"id":"webhook-make","h":"Webhook Make","x":"Creating the Make Scenario Once connected to Make, create a scenario. Then create a Custom Webhook module as follows: Click on the Add button. Once the Webhook module is configured, simply click on the Copy address to clipboard button. Creating the Screen This screen prompts the user for their name to send it to Make via the Webhook. The screen includes: A Text input field whose variable is called Your name. A button that triggers the Make Webhook via an action. A LabelText component that displays the response from the Webhook stored in the variable Result. Creating the Action Select the button, then click on its Action property. Drag and drop the Make Webhook action and configure the action as follows: Set the Variable property to Result. Set the Webhook URL property to the URL of your Webhook copied from your Make scenario. The Request data contains a data item called name that refers to the variable Your name. Thus, the Make Webhook will receive the parameter of the user's name entered in the Zyllio screen. Testing the Webhook Return to the Make scenario and click on the Run once button. The Make scenario is waiting for a call from Zyllio. From Zyllio, go into simulation mode an"}]},{"u":"en/monetize-saas-apps-89.html","t":"Monetize your SaaS apps (SaaS)","x":"Monetize your SaaS apps (SaaS) Available from the Enterprise plan","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Introduction","x":"This tutorial video explains the different steps to set up a sales page to monetize your applications. This sales page uses the Zyllio SaaS actions to create applications from your templates and publish them"}]},{"u":"en/offline-mode-62.html","t":"Offline Mode","x":"The Offline mode of your mobile application allows users to use the app without an internet connection.  Available from the Enterprise plan. This mode is particularly useful for users in areas with unstable or nonexistent connectivity. Here are the detailed steps to activate and use Offline mode.","s":[{"id":"activating-offline-mode","h":"Activating Offline Mode","x":"From Zyllio Studio, open your mobile application and navigate to the Settings section. You will find an option titled Offline; toggle the Enable button to allow the application to function without an internet connection. Simply activating Offline mode is not enough to start using the application offline. An initial synchronization is required to download the necessary data for offline use."},{"id":"data-synchronization","h":"Data Synchronization","x":"Once Offline mode is activated, it is essential to synchronize the data to use the application properly offline. This synchronization downloads the necessary data so that it is available without a connection. A synchronization button is needed, and this button can be placed on the screen that you find most convenient for the user, such as the home screen or a specific data management screen. The synchronization button uses the Offline sync action, which does not require any special configuration."},{"id":"using-offline-mode","h":"Using Offline Mode","x":"Click the Synchronize (or Sync) button to initiate the data synchronization. Once this operation is complete, all the necessary information for using the application will be stored locally on your device. After synchronization, you will see a confirmation message indicating that the synchronization was successful. You can now use the application without an internet connection. When the application is connected to the network, Offline mode remains operational. However, it prioritizes online information when available and updates its offline cache accordingly."},{"id":"limitations","h":"Limitations","x":"Currently, automatic updates of the application in Offline mode are not supported. If you have an urgent need for specific updates or a feature related to updates in Offline mode, we encourage you to submit your request directly to the Product Manager via the dedicated product Slack forum. Visit forum"}]},{"u":"en/plugins-36.html","t":"Plugins","x":"Zyllio offers the ability to extend and customize its interface and features through plugins. These plugins are developed using an SDK available on GitHub. Plugin development is primarily based on TypeScript, which is a superset of JavaScript that adds a static typing system. Zyllio's SDK fully supports TypeScript, allowing developers to write more structured and secure code while benefiting from strong typing. Zyllio's GitHub SDK also provides documentation, code examples, and tools for testing and debugging plugins during development. Developers can clone the GitHub repository, explore the provided examples, and adapt them to their needs to quickly build functional plugins. Plugins can include different types of custom components: Visual component**: An interface or visual element to display. Action: A function triggered by a user. Formula: A custom function for calculations or automations. Theme: A customization of the application's overall appearance (colors, fonts, etc.).","s":[{"id":"prerequisites-for-plugin-development","h":"Prerequisites for Plugin Development","x":"Knowledge of JavaScript/TypeScript. Access to the Zyllio SDK on GitHub: https://github.com/zyllio/zyllio-sdk . Development tools such as Node.js for JavaScript/TypeScript development."},{"id":"installing-a-plugin-in-zyllio","h":"Installing a Plugin in Zyllio","x":"Once the plugin development is complete, you need to install it in Zyllio via the dedicated interface in the application's settings. After installing the plugin, it is important to click the \"RELOAD PLUGINS\" button to ensure the system applies the new changes and properly activates your plugin."},{"id":"using-your-plugin","h":"Using Your Plugin","x":"Depending on the nature of the plugin, you will find your component, action, or formula in a \"Plugins\" section. These new elements can be used just like any native Zyllio elements. Plugins are installed at the application level in Zyllio. Each application can have its own set of plugins"}]},{"u":"en/publications-5.html","t":"Publications","x":"You can publish your application at any time, even if your application is not completely finished and fully functional.","s":[{"id":"publish-your-application","h":"Publish Your Application","x":"The publishing process is accessible from the main toolbar. It allows you to quickly publish a mobile application and make it immediately available to users. The application can be downloaded onto a mobile phone via the displayed QR code or opened in desktop mode on a PC or Mac. Additionally, the application's URL can be copied for easier sharing."},{"id":"sharing-screen","h":"Sharing Screen","x":"Download via QR Code Once the application is published, a unique QR code is generated. Users can scan this code with their mobile phone's camera to download and install the application directly onto their device. This method is quick and does not require access to an app store (such as Google Play or the App Store) to complete the installation. Desktop Mode Access The application can also be opened and used in desktop mode on a PC or Mac. This allows for testing or browsing the app on a larger screen. The \"Open desktop\" button displayed on the screen allows you to directly open the application in a compatible browser on a computer. URL Sharing A \"Copy link\" button allows you to copy the application's URL to the clipboard. This makes it easy to share the link with other users, whether by email, message, or social media. The link will redirect users to the application, either on their mobile device or computer, depending on the device they are using. Using a Custom Domain If the application has a custom domain configured, the generated URL for sharing will use the custom domain instead of a default generic URL. This feature is useful for branding the application with a trusted domain"}]},{"u":"en/push-notifications-17.html","t":"Push Notifications","x":"Push Notifications are messages sent directly to a mobile device, even when the application is not open or running.  Available from the Enterprise plan. They are an essential tool for keeping your users informed in real-time, reminding them of important events, or encouraging them to return to the app. This feature is available on both iOS and Android, providing direct communication with your users on the two main platforms. Follow these steps to activate and configure push notifications for your project, whether on iOS or Android. Push notifications are well supported on Android, but their reliability may fluctuate depending on the manufacturer and the version of the operating system.","s":[{"id":"activating-push-notifications","h":"Activating Push Notifications","x":"To enable push notifications in your application, go to the Settings section, then navigate to the Push Notifications section, and finally click on the Enable button."},{"id":"activating-push-notifications-in-the-mobile-app","h":"Activating Push Notifications in the Mobile App","x":"Push notifications also need to be enabled in the mobile app for each user. To do this, you should include a dedicated screen of your choice in the app that asks users for permission to receive notifications. Create a dedicated screen in your application that explains to users why they should allow notifications. Add a button to enable users to subscribe to notifications. Use the Subscribe action, which allows the user to opt-in to receive notifications. This action will automatically request permission from the user. The Subscribe action has two optional properties: User and Channel. These properties allow you to configure how notifications are received, targeting a specific user or a group of users. Details of the properties: User: If this property is set, it means that the current user will receive personalized notifications intended for them. Channel: If you want to send notifications to a group of users, you can set this property with a channel name (e.g., premium subscribers, visitors, internal users, etc.). All users subscribed to this channel will receive the notifications. Empty Parameters: If both properties are left empty, the user will be subscribed to receive all gener"},{"id":"sending-notifications-to-users","h":"Sending Notifications to Users","x":"Once your users are subscribed, you can send them notifications based on their channel subscription or individually. The user must have the application installed in order to receive push notifications. Here are the actions you can use to send notifications: Send to all : Use this action to send a notification to all users who have enabled push notifications. Send to channel : With this action, you can send notifications only to users subscribed to a specific channel. This allows you to segment notifications according to specific user groups. Send to user : Use this action to send a notification to a specific user who has enabled notifications."},{"id":"example-of-using-notifications","h":"Example of Using Notifications","x":"If you want to inform all your users about an update, use Send to All . To send a notification only to your premium users, define a channel named \"premium\" and use Send to Channel with that channel. To notify a specific user about an action in the app, use Send to User and specify, for example, their email."}]},{"u":"en/rest-integration-52.html","t":"REST Integration","x":"This integration allows you to connect an external data source to your Zyllio application.  Available from the Enterprise plan. This external system must expose a REST API, which is the current standard for data exchange between systems. This integration enables you to query, create, and update external data.","s":[{"id":"configurations","h":"Configurations","x":"It is necessary to provide the configurations for your REST integrations from the Settings tab, then Integrations. Enable the REST integration by clicking on Enable. Zyllio allows you to define as many REST configurations as needed. A REST configuration enables you to set the parameters for the REST service (Endpoint URL, Headers, etc.). A first integration with XANO. A second integration with BigQuery. A third integration with Instagram."},{"id":"defining-a-rest-configuration","h":"Defining a REST Configuration","x":"Click on the + button to add a configuration. The following parameters are required. Parameter Example value Name Retrieve memes Endpoint URL https://api.imgflip.com/get_memes Headers Authorization: Bearer AZERTYUIOP1234567890"},{"id":"triggering-a-rest-request","h":"Triggering a REST Request","x":"Actions are available in the REST section of the action editor. There is one action for each type of request: GET, PATCH, PUT, POST, DELETE. Query Request The Get Request action allows you to query an external data source. The following parameters are available. Parameter Example value Variable meme REST Configuration Retrieve memes Additional path (empty) Request parameters (empty) Response expression data.memes[0] Response Expression A response expression allows you to select the data you want to extract from the JSON response. The following public REST service: https://api.imgflip.com/get_memes returns this JSON structure. A response expression allows you to select only the data that the mobile application needs. Creation Request The Post Request action allows the REST service to create a data or data structure. Update Request The Put Request action allows the REST service to update a data or data structure. Delete Request The Delete Request action allows the REST service to delete a data or data structure. Partial Update Request The Patch Request action allows the REST service to update a single property of a data. It is not necessary to provide all the properties of that data."},{"id":"display-a-rest-data","h":"Display a REST Data","x":"Simple Data The data stored by the REST action can be simple, especially through the use of a response expression. For example: 'Paris', 'True', or 123. In this case, the component on the screen can refer to this data directly. Structured Data The data stored by the REST action can be structured and can contain properties, for example, a meme with all its properties: id, name, and url. In this case, a formula that uses the JSON Query function allows you to select the properties to display. Below, the LabelText component calls a formula that uses the JSON Query function. This function defines two properties. Property Value JSON object meme Expression name Here is the screen in operation in the simulator. Once the REST request is executed, the JSON Query function can be used as many times as needed without generating additional REST requests."}]},{"u":"en/scenarios-96.html","t":"Scenarios","x":"Scenarios (workflows) automate processing in your application: sending an email, updating data, calling an external service, etc. Each scenario is made of a trigger that defines when it starts, and a sequence of steps (actions) that define what it does.","s":[{"id":"the-scenarios-space","h":"The Scenarios space","x":"Scenarios are managed from the Scenarios tab of the design tool. The left panel lists the application's scenarios. A search field and filters ( All , Click , Planned , Webhook , Table ) help you find a scenario quickly. The trigger is displayed under each scenario name, for example Planned - Daily (12:00) . The right panel displays the steps of the selected scenario. The Actions button adds steps, which are then linked together starting from the Start block. When hovering a scenario, buttons let you, depending on its type, rename or edit it, run it manually, view its execution history, display its usages or delete it. Available actions are detailed in the Actions page."},{"id":"create-a-scenario","h":"Create a scenario","x":"Click Create scenario at the bottom of the list, then enter a name and choose a trigger. Trigger Description User click Starts when someone taps or clicks inside the app Planned run Runs automatically at a chosen time or rhythm Webhook Launches when an external service sends a request Table event Starts when a row is created, updated or deleted in a table Depending on the selected trigger, a configuration window opens to enter the scenario name and its settings."},{"id":"user-click","h":"User click","x":"A Click scenario is a shared scenario: it can be reused from several screens or from other scenarios, instead of duplicating the same actions. The Usages button lists the screens and scenarios that use it. A shared scenario that is still in use cannot be deleted. Remove its usages first."},{"id":"planned-run","h":"Planned run","x":"A scheduled scenario runs automatically according to a frequency. Frequency Settings Example Hourly - Will run every hour. Daily Hour (0-23) Will run every day at 12:00. Weekly Day of the week, Hour Will run every Monday at 8:00. Monthly Day of the month (1-31), Hour Will run every month on day 1 at 6:00. Scheduled scenarios run at the top of the hour. The Run manually button lets you test a scheduled scenario without waiting for its next trigger. With a monthly frequency, a day that does not exist in the month (for example the 31st) is not executed that month."},{"id":"table-event","h":"Table event","x":"A Table scenario starts automatically when data changes in one of the application's tables. Choose the Table to watch, then the Event : Any change Row created Row updated Row deleted"},{"id":"webhook","h":"Webhook","x":"A Webhook scenario is triggered by an external system (ERP, CRM, script, another application...) sending an HTTP POST request to Zyllio. Webhook URL The URL is generated automatically and displayed in the webhook configuration window. It looks like this: https://<your-application-host>/api/workflow/<application-id>/<scenario-id> API key Each request must include the x-api-key header with the application's API key. This key is located in the Settings tab, Integrations section, API card. Click the key to copy it. The API key gives access to your application. Do not share it and do not embed it in code running in a browser. Variables Variables define the data expected in the JSON body of the request. Each variable has a unique name and a type. Type Expected JSON value Example Text String \"Smith\" Number Number 42.5 Boolean true or false true Date ISO 8601 string \"2026-11-02\" or \"2026-11-02T14:30:00Z\" All declared variables are required. The received values can then be used in the scenario steps. Call example The configuration window provides ready-to-copy code examples (curl, wget, fetch, PowerShell). Example with curl: curl -X POST \"https://<host>/api/workflow/<application-id>/<scenar"},{"id":"execution-history","h":"Execution history","x":"Scheduled, Webhook and Table scenarios have an execution history, available from the Execution history button in the list. Executions can be filtered ( All , Success , Failed ). Selecting an execution displays its details: the execution ID and status the queue waiting time the trigger variables (payload) the console logs of the execution The Delete all history button clears the scenario history. This action cannot be undone."}]},{"u":"en/screen-editor-8.html","t":"Screen editor","x":"The screen editor provides a user-friendly interface that allows users to create and customize screens for their mobile applications. This section briefly introduces the tool's objectives and highlights its main features.","s":[{"id":"screen-creation","h":"Screen Creation","x":"Screen creation is available from the 'Screen' toolbar, which displays predefined templates to speed up the creation process. Template Description Blank Create a blank screen Header Create a screen with a title bar Info Create an informative screen with some text, an image, and a button Welcome Create a welcome screen with some text, an image, and a button Contact Create a contact screen with contact buttons: Twitter, Website, Phone, Email, and YouTube Channel Booking Create a screen that contains a registration form Login Create a preconfigured authentication screen with Email and Password fields and an authentication button Sign up Create a user signup screen with Name, email, password, profile image fields and a signup button List Create a screen that displays the content of a table as a grid list Details Create a screen that displays the details of a selection, each field of the selection being displayed according to its type. A Text component for the text type, a Phone component for the phonenumber type, a Map Marker component for the location type ... Update Create a form-type screen that allows modifying each field of the selection. A FormText component for the text type, a "},{"id":"modification-of-the-screens","h":"Modification of the screens","x":"Workspace Management Spatial Organization of Screens Align the screens precisely to ensure an efficient layout. Use arrangement tools to optimize space and improve readability. Manipulation Tools Move and resize screens with intuitive tools. Drag and Drop of Visual Components Adding Components to Screens Drag and drop visual components from the library onto the screens. Choose from a variety of elements such as buttons, text fields, images, lists, etc. Component Configuration Customize the properties of each component according to your needs. Adjust settings such as color, size, and interactive behavior. Selection, Copy, and Paste Selecting Screens and Components Learn different methods for efficiently selecting screens and components. Use shortcuts to speed up the selection process: CTRL+A to select all screens. Copy-Paste Quickly duplicate screens and components to save time. Copy elements from one screen to another."},{"id":"defining-the-application-theme","h":"Defining the Application Theme","x":"Customization of appearance Select colors and other graphic elements to define the theme. Real-time preview of changes for instant visualization. Colors Name Description Primary Color Primary color used for the background of the title bar (header) Primary Text Color Primary color used for the text in the title bar (header) Secondary Color Secondary color used for the background of list item selections and buttons Secondary Text Color Secondary color used for the text of list item selections and buttons Tertiary Color Tertiary color used for the background of input fields, often semi-transparent Terciary Text Color Tertiary color used for the text in input fields Background Color Default color used for the background of screens Text Color Default color used for the text of components Properties Name Description Right to left Toggle text display from right to left, useful for certain languages (e.g., Arabic) Theme Application Easily apply the defined theme across the entire application. Ability to change the theme at any time based on preferences."}]},{"u":"en/screen-sequence-editor-23.html","t":"Screen sequence editor","x":"This editor allows you to link the screens of your mobile application based on user interactions, such as button clicks and other graphical components like lists. You will also learn how to set the startup screen of your application.","s":[{"id":"linking-screens","h":"Linking screens","x":"To create smooth navigation in your application, follow these steps: Add an interactive component Add a graphical component (for example, an Enter button) to your screen as shown below. Set a screen transition In the Flow tab Select the button component Drag the right handle to the destination screen Download the video file Undo a screen transition In the Flow tab Select the button component Select the link Drag the end of the link outside the screen Download the video file"},{"id":"set-the-startup-screen","h":"Set the startup screen","x":"To set the screen that appears when the application is launched: Go back to the screen sequence editor. Locate the screen you want to set as the startup screen. Click the home icon to the left of that screen."},{"id":"test-your-navigation","h":"Test your navigation","x":"It is important to test your application's navigation to ensure everything works as expected. Use the simulator mode from the Simulation tab. Interact with the screens you have configured. Check if the navigation meets your expectations."},{"id":"tips-and-tricks","h":"Tips and tricks","x":"Ensure that the transitions between screens are logical and intuitive for the user. Use clear screen names to simplify navigation configuration. Regularly test changes to avoid navigation errors."}]},{"u":"en/simulation-6.html","t":"Simulation","x":"The simulator allows you to test mobile applications in a simulated environment, providing the ability to visualize and interact with applications on different mobile phone models. It supports both Android and iOS platforms. This mobile application simulator is an efficient tool for testing your application's features on various devices and operating systems. It also simplifies the testing process by offering quick authentication through automatic login. Use it to check the performance and user experience of your applications under conditions close to real-life scenarios.","s":[{"id":"device-model-selection","h":"Device Model Selection","x":"The simulator offers a wide range of device models from which you can choose to test your application. You can select popular Android and iOS phones, such as Samsung Galaxy and iPhones. To change the device model, use the dropdown menu on the left side of the interface. This adjusts the application's display according to the chosen device's specifications, including screen resolution and operating system specifics."},{"id":"automatic-authentication","h":"Automatic Authentication","x":"The simulator features an automatic authentication function, allowing you to log into the application without manually entering an email and password. To use this feature, you need to select a user to authenticate from the dedicated menu. This option simulates quick login to an application without having to enter login credentials each time. For automatic authentication to work correctly, the authentication table must contain three required columns: Email: The user's email Name: The user's name Password: The user's password If these columns are not present, the automatic authentication feature cannot be used. Therefore, it is essential to check the table structure before enabling this option."},{"id":"how-to-use-the-simulator","h":"How to Use the Simulator","x":"Device Selection Access the dropdown menu at the top left to choose a phone model. Once selected, the simulator will automatically adjust the display to match the appearance of that device. Automatic Authentication To enable automatic authentication, select the corresponding option in the \"Act as\" menu and choose a predefined user. This will simulate the experience of a user logging into the application directly without manually entering their credentials."}]},{"u":"en/sso-90.html","t":"SSO","x":"SSO Available from the Enterprise plan","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Introduction","x":"Single Sign-On (SSO) lets users sign in to Zyllio using their usual login credentials. This secure method centralizes access management and simplifies the user experience."},{"id":"definitions","h":"Definitions","x":"Identity Provider (the client system) : The platform that manages user identities (for example: Microsoft Entra ID/Azure, Okta, Google Workspace). It authenticates the user before sending them to Zyllio. Service Provider (Zyllio) : The final application that provides the service. Zyllio trusts the information sent by the client system. Metadata : An XML file or text that contains the certificates and settings required to establish a secure link between the client system and Zyllio."},{"id":"configuration-procedure","h":"Configuration procedure","x":"Activation The first step is to turn on the Enable switch. This action unlocks the configuration fields and allows Zyllio to process SAML2 login requests. Declaring Zyllio in the client system For the client system to accept connecting users to Zyllio, it must know our application settings: Check the Service Provider section on this page. Click the DOWNLOAD FILE button to retrieve Zyllio metadata. Action: this file must be imported into the client system administration interface to create the link. Linking the client system to Zyllio Once the client-side setup is complete, the client system generates its own metadata so that Zyllio can recognize it: Retrieve the XML metadata text provided by the client system. Paste this text into the field provided under the Identity Provider section. Once saved, the configuration is operational. Deployment tips Verification: Perform a login test in a private browsing window to confirm that the flow works correctly. Continuity: Make sure an administrator account keeps a standard access method (email/password) while you validate the SSO setup to avoid any accidental lockout. Security: these files are not \"secrets\" Unlike a password or a private API"},{"id":"authentication-screens","h":"Authentication screens","x":"User management For the login flow to work, Zyllio must know which internal account corresponds to the user signing in through SSO: Users table: A users table must be configured in Zyllio. Unique identifier: This table must contain an Email column. This email address is used as the matching key: once authentication succeeds on the client side, Zyllio looks up the corresponding user by email and opens their session. Available actions Once configured, two actions are available to manage SSO login: Authentication : Securely signs in the user after verification by the client system. Logout : Properly ends the user's session in Zyllio."}]},{"u":"en/stripe-payments-69.html","t":"Stripe Payments","x":"Stripe is an online payment infrastructure that allows businesses to manage and accept payments securely.  Available from the Enterprise plan. The Zyllio platform does not charge any fees on payments made through Stripe. By integrating Zyllio with Stripe, you will benefit from its robust features, such as payment management, recurring billing, fraud protection, and much more.","s":[{"id":"prerequisites","h":"Prerequisites","x":"A valid Stripe account is required to connect the Stripe and Zyllio solutions. Additionally, a subscription to the Zyllio Enterprise plan is needed to activate this feature."},{"id":"configuration","h":"Configuration","x":"The Stripe integration can be activated from the Settings tab / Integration / Stripe by enabling the Enable button as shown in this image. Connect Your Stripe Account Next, click the Connect with Stripe button to link your account with the Zyllio platform. Log in and click on Submit. Finally, click the Connect button. Close the tab when the following message appears: \"Your Stripe account has been connected. Please close this window.\" Test and Production Mode We recommend conducting all your Zyllio application design and payment testing in Test Mode. During the testing period, the Production Mode option should be disabled. Production mode can be enabled once the application is finalized. Currency The currency parameter allows you to specify the currency for payments in your mobile application. Currency name Currency Dollar USD Euro EUR"},{"id":"creating-products","h":"Creating Products","x":"From the Database tab, click and drag a Products table into the workspace. This way, Zyllio creates a ready-to-use table with 7 sample products."},{"id":"creating-catalog-screens","h":"Creating Catalog Screens","x":"From the Designer tab, select the List screen template and then select the Products table. Click and drag the screen to create this first screen, then rename the screen to Products. Next, create a detail screen by selecting a Details screen template, then choose the Products screen, and finally select the Products component. Click and drag the screen to create this new screen, then rename it to Details. Remove the Currency and PriceId components from the screen. Change the Price component to a Formula, then select the Currency format and the € symbol. Modify the Price component and change the Label property to Price. And link the two screens from the Flow tab."},{"id":"authentication","h":"Authentication","x":"Create an authentication screen using a Login screen template and then select the Users table. Next, link the Enter button to the Products screen. Designate the Login screen as the startup screen by clicking on the icon as shown below."},{"id":"creating-a-cart","h":"Creating a Cart","x":"Add an Add to Cart button in the Details screen. Define an Add Product action and fill in the following properties. Property Type Value Image Variable Products/Image Price Id Variable Products/PriceId Name Variable Products/Name Description Variable Products/Description Price Variable Products/Price Currency Variable Products/Currency Quantity Fixed 1 Next, create a screen called Cart to display the cart. Select a Cart component by choosing Fixed. Drag the component into the screen as follows. Link the Add To Cart button to the Cart screen."},{"id":"creating-a-payment-button","h":"Creating a Payment Button","x":"Add a button called Pay that uses the Process Payment action."},{"id":"define-the-price-source","h":"Define the price source","x":"The screen below illustrates the configuration of the Stripe integration in your application. The elements highlighted in yellow emphasize the configuration of the price source, which is essential for linking your database data to Stripe. Here is an explanation of the different fields: Price Table, Price ID Column, Price Column. Field Description Example Price Table Select the table in your database that contains information about your products or services. This table serves as a reference for the prices that Stripe will use during transactions. Products Price ID Column Select the column that contains the unique identifiers for the prices (Price ID). These identifiers allow Stripe to link the prices defined in your database to those configured in your Stripe account. PriceId (e.g. price_12345) Price Column Select the column that contains the price values. This column defines the amount users will pay for a product or service. Price (e.g. 10.00) At this stage, your application is ready to accept payments in test mode."},{"id":"testing-a-payment","h":"Testing a Payment","x":"When conducting interactive tests, use a card number of type: 4242 4242 4242 4242. Use a valid expiration date such as 12/34. Use any three-digit CVC code (four digits for American Express cards). Use any value of your choice for the other fields in the form. Payments appear in the Transactions section."},{"id":"reconciling-in-zyllio","h":"Reconciling in Zyllio","x":"Zyllio captures successful payments in a specific table called Orders. Simply create it from the table templates in the Database tab. Here is an example of a successful transaction."},{"id":"displaying-user-orders","h":"Displaying User Orders","x":"Simply create a new screen called Orders that lists the contents of the Orders table. A filter is needed to display only the orders of the authenticated user."},{"id":"errors","h":"Errors","x":"Error Stripe message Reason Payment failed, code 01 The `price` parameter should be the ID of a price object, rather than the literal numerical price. Please see https://stripe.com/docs/billing/prices-guide#create-prices for more information about how to set up price objects. One or more products have an undeclared priceId in the Stripe catalog Payment failed, code 01 The price specified supports currencies of `eur` which doesn't include the expected currency of `usd`. A product currency does not match the Stripe integration currency in Zyllio Settings Payment failed, code 02 Requires payment method The payment method is not valid Payment failed, code 03 Status unknown Should not occur"}]},{"u":"en/timetonic-88.html","t":"TimeTonic","x":"TimeTonic Zyllio lets you easily connect your TimeTonic databases to an application without needing programming knowledge. Thanks to this integration, you can access, view, and interact with your TimeTonic data in real time, directly from your application.","s":[{"id":"configuration","h":"Configuration","x":"TimeTonic setup Before you start importing your TimeTonic tables and views, you must configure the TimeTonic integration Here are the steps to follow Sign in to your TimeTonic account Open your account settings Then select the Settings option in the left menu Click the Add a new API key button and select the options below Validate the API key creation Once created, click Copy to copy the API key Also note your account identifier from Account Information Zyllio setup Sign in to Zyllio Select the application to integrate with TimeTonic Select the Settings tab, then TimeTonic Enable the TimeTonic integration Paste the API key copied from the TimeTonic portal and the user identifier as follows Importing TimeTonic tables and views From the Database tab, click the TimeTonic option in the Import table section on the left Then select the table or tables to import. This operation can be repeated later to import other tables Once validated, Zyllio imports all selected tables as well as field types and their relationships. Example of 5 imported tables with relationships Calculated fields such as formulas, lookup, rollup, and id ... are marked with a lock. These fields cannot be edited from Zy"},{"id":"structure-synchronization","h":"Structure synchronization","x":"When you make structural changes to your TimeTonic tables, for example adding a field, these changes do not appear in Zyllio To synchronize these changes, click the Refresh columns button. Once synchronization starts, Zyllio will add and remove fields based on the changes Zyllio never modifies the structure of TimeTonic tables in your TimeTonic account. Only the Zyllio definition can be modified from Zyllio"},{"id":"data-synchronization","h":"Data synchronization","x":"Zyllio uses a data cache to improve access performance to TimeTonic based on how the TimeTonic tables are used. As a result, changes from TimeTonic or an external automation may not be visible immediately in Zyllio. A delay of up to 1 minute may be necessary To force a refresh, click the Refresh data button"}]},{"u":"en/transfer-an-application-82.html","t":"Transfer an application","x":"Transfer an application This page presents the different steps to follow to transfer an application. You will find practical information and advice to make the handoff easier, ensure project continuity, and guarantee the satisfaction of everyone involved.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Access the Zyllio Studio","x":"Access the Zyllio Studio through this link: https://www.zyllio.one Sign in with your Google account to display the Zyllio dashboard below"},{"id":"subscribe-to-the-enterprise-plan","h":"Subscribe to the Enterprise plan","x":"Click the button located in the top left corner Select the Enterprise subscription Proceed with the payment; navigation returns automatically to your Zyllio dashboard with your subscription immediately activated"},{"id":"transfer-an-application","h":"Transfer an application","x":"Access your shared applications from the Shared menu as follows: Click the application menu, then Clone Congratulations, your application is now available in your account. Return to the All menu to access your applications When you clone your application, you get an independent copy that belongs exclusively to you and is not accessible to any other user unless you choose to share it"},{"id":"support","h":"Support","x":"Do not hesitate to contact us by email: contact@zyllio.com via Slack: https://join.slack.com/t/zyllio-studio/shared_invite/zt-1etbsnzqp-S5UecnkGZVzn0YmYO9yFJw"}]},{"u":"en/translations-76.html","t":"Translations","x":"Translations The translation of a mobile application is a key element to reach an international audience and provide a personalized user experience. This documentation guides you step by step to implement a multilingual translation system in your application.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Use the \"Translations\" table for labels","x":"To manage translations, Zyllio offers a predefined table named Translations . This table is located in the Database tab and already contains examples of translated labels to help you get started. Structure of the \"Translations\" table The Translations table is organized as follows: First column : The name of the label. Each label must be unique and represents the text you want to display in your application. Following columns : Each column represents a language, according to the ISO standard (for example: fr for French, en for English, es for Spanish, etc.). These columns contain the translations of the corresponding label. Example of Translations table Label name fr en es welcome_message Bienvenue Welcome Bienvenido button_submit Soumettre Submit Enviar error_not_found Not found Not found No encontrado Fill this table with the labels and their translations for all the languages supported by your application. Example of Translations table"},{"id":"use-the-translate-formula-in-components","h":"Use the \"Translate\" formula in components","x":"Once the Translations table is completed, you can integrate the translations into your application using the Translate formula. This formula is used in display components (such as texts, buttons, etc.) present on your screens. Properties of the \"Translate\" formula Translations table : Reference the Translations table. Label name : Specify the label name as defined in the first column of the table. Translate Formula usage When the application is launched, the user's language is automatically detected. This means that if the user has set their device to French, the labels in French will be displayed by default. If the user's language is not supported, the first language defined in the Translations table will be used."}]},{"u":"en/tutoriels-68.html","t":"Tutorials","x":"A practical learning path to master integrations, automation, and advanced app capabilities.","s":[{"id":"recommended-learning-path","h":"Recommended learning path","x":"Start with data basics: Google Sheets and Airtable . Continue with logic and state: Formulas and Variables . Add external services: REST Integration and Authentication . Scale engagement: Push Notifications , Chat , and Stripe Payments ."},{"id":"advanced-topics","h":"Advanced topics","x":"Offline Mode for low-connectivity contexts. Custom domain for branded web deployment. Translations for multilingual apps. Xano , Make , and Zapier for automation pipelines."}]},{"u":"en/twilio-14.html","t":"Twilio","x":"Available from the Professional plan The Twilio integration allows you to easily send WhatsApp messages and SMS. By simply entering your Twilio account information, you can communicate with your users.","s":[{"id":"configuring-twilio","h":"Configuring Twilio","x":"Prerequisites Before you begin, make sure you have an active Twilio account with available credits. If you haven't done so yet, sign up on the Twilio website and add credits to your account. Generating API Keys To configure Twilio, you will need your Account SID and Auth Token. You can obtain them by following these steps: Log in to your Twilio account at https://console.twilio.com/ Go to the Account Info section on your dashboard. Note down your Account SID and Auth Token."},{"id":"zyllio-configuration","h":"Zyllio Configuration","x":"Open your application from the Zyllio Studio. Go to the Settings tab. Select the Twilio section. Enable the integration by clicking on the Enable option. Copy and paste your Account SID and Auth Token into the corresponding fields."},{"id":"using-messaging-actions","h":"Using Messaging Actions","x":"Sending WhatsApp Messages Go to the action editor from a button defined on a screen. Select the Send WhatsApp action. Enter the sender's phone number (from phone number). Enter the recipient's phone number (to phone number) in international format, e.g., +1234567890. Specify the message you wish to send (message) Sending SMS Go to the action editor from a button defined on a screen. Select the Send SMS action. Enter the sender's phone number (from phone number). Enter the recipient's phone number (to phone number) in international format, e.g., +1234567890. Specify the message you wish to send (message)."},{"id":"important-notes","h":"Important Notes","x":"The Send WhatsApp and Send SMS actions submit the sending request to Twilio. The Zyllio action is considered successful if the request is properly transmitted, but this does not guarantee that the message is successfully sent by Twilio. If in doubt, check their statuses in the Twilio Monitoring interface. Note that some messages may be rejected by the platforms. For example: Twilio Error 63040 ."}]},{"u":"en/variables-54.html","t":"Variables","x":"Variables In the context of a no-code tool, a variable is a fundamental element used to store and manipulate data. Variables can be thought of as containers in which you can store information such as numbers, text, or more complex data. Their main advantage is that they can change value during the execution of your application.","s":[{"id":"welcome","h":"Creation of variables","x":"There are 2 types of variables: Simple variables based on user input or action results Selection variables based on lists Input Components You can create simple variables using input components, such as a text input field. For example, a user can enter their name in an input field, and that entry is then stored in a variable. All the following components store input in simple variables Actions Actions can be used to store the result of a process in a simple variable. Action Commentary Get Current Position Retrieves the GPS position of the device and stores it in a simple variable. Increment Increments a simple variable and stores it again. Scan QR Code The scanned code is stored in a simple variable. Selection Components List-type components such as grids (Grid), lists (List), or carousels (Carousel) can be used to define selection variables. When a user selects an item from these components, the selected item is assigned to such a variable."},{"id":"using-variables","h":"Using Variables","x":"Variables can be used on any screen. A variable created in one screen can be read on any other screen, not necessarily the same screen. Reading and Displaying All visual components, formulas, and actions in your application can read and display the values stored in variables. This provides great flexibility in presenting and processing data. Additionally, variables can be combined using formulas to build texts that contain both fixed words and variable data. Variables can be formatted according to their type to customize their display. Type Format Nombres Number of decimals Integer Montants Currencies Dates Date/Time/Date and Time Short or long format Manipulation by Actions Most actions allow variables to be specified in their properties for execution. For example, the Navigate action defines a property called URL; this property can have a fixed value (for example https://zyllio.com ) or take a previously stored variable (for example web site). Some actions produce a value and store it in a new variable or an existing variable."},{"id":"best-naming-practices","h":"Best Naming Practices","x":"It is recommended to name variables with a meaningful name based on their use: Use \"Selected Product\" on a product list Use \"Scanned Code\" on a Scan QR Code action An application that contains dozens of variables can be extremely difficult to maintain over the long term. If the application contains dozens of screens using the same data sources, it is recommended to extend names with more context. For example \"All products\" and \"Favorite products\". Avoid the following names: acronyms numbered variables such as \"variable 1\", \"variable 2\" shortened names"}]},{"u":"en/xano-75.html","t":"Xano","x":"This documentation guides you through the necessary steps to integrate Xano.  Available from the Enterprise plan You will learn how to configure Xano and use its tables to power your applications. Xano is a backend platform that allows you to create, manage, and leverage a database with powerful APIs. When paired with Zyllio, Xano provides a robust solution for creating efficient and complex applications.","s":[{"id":"configure-xano","h":"Configure Xano","x":"Table Configuration In the Database section, create your tables according to the needs of your application. For example, a Students table. API Configuration Access the Settings section of your workspace, then Manage. Then, create a variable called API_KEY as follows. You can set the key value as you wish. It is often recommended to use a key in the form of a complex alphanumeric string for added security. For example: a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-gh12-ijklmnop3456 Configure Endpoints To access Xano data from Zyllio, it is necessary to create 4 endpoints in Xano. If your application does not perform any modification operations, you can only set the endpoint type to GET. Here are the steps applicable to the 4 endpoints. Add an endpoint. Select CRUD database operations. Select the table. Select one of the endpoint types and click on Save."},{"id":"secure-your-endpoints","h":"Secure Your Endpoints","x":"Open the details of the endpoint to be secured, and click the Add function button. Click on Add function. Select Utility Functions. Select Precondition. Click on Add Condition and input the following parameters. Remember to publish your endpoint and copy its URL for the following steps."},{"id":"using-xano-tables-in-zyllio","h":"Using Xano Tables in Zyllio","x":"Once your application is created, select Settings then REST. Click Enable to activate REST exchanges with other solutions such as Xano. Add a configuration called Xano, for example. Specify the URL of the endpoint previously copied from the Xano interface and the API key defined in Xano. Example."},{"id":"import-the-xano-table","h":"Import the Xano Table","x":"From the Database tab, click import then REST. Then select the Students endpoint and click Import. You get a table where all the columns correspond to those in Xano."},{"id":"display-the-xano-table","h":"Display the Xano Table","x":"You can view and modify the content of the Xano table just like any table defined in your application. Let's use a List screen to display the Students table. Then select the Students table. You get:"}]},{"u":"en/zapier-71.html","t":"Zapier","x":"Zapier is an online automation tool that connects your applications and services to automate repetitive tasks without the need for coding.  Available from the Enterprise plan. It works by linking two or more applications to create automations, called Zaps, which trigger an action in one application when something happens in another. Zyllio offers a native integration with Zapier that allows: Triggering Zaps from Zyllio (via Webhook), Updating Zyllio data from Zapier Zaps.","s":[{"id":"prerequisites","h":"Prerequisites","x":"A valid Zapier account is required to connect the Zapier and Zyllio solutions. Additionally, a subscription to the Zyllio Enterprise plan is needed to activate this feature."},{"id":"configuration","h":"Configuration","x":"The Zapier integration can be activated from the Settings tab / Integration / Zapier by enabling the Enable button as shown in this image."},{"id":"zapier-webhook","h":"Zapier Webhook","x":"Creating the Zapier Scenario Once connected to Zapier, click on New Zap to create a Zap. Then, select Webhooks by Zapier. Select the Event: Catch Hook and click on Continue. Click Continue again. The Webhook step is now created. This screen allows you to copy the URL of your Webhook by clicking the Copy button. Creating the Screen This screen prompts the user for their name to send it to Zapier via the Webhook. The screen includes: A Text input field whose variable is called Your name. A button that triggers the Zapier Webhook via an action. A LabelText component that displays the response from the Webhook stored in the variable Result. Creating the Action Select the button, then click on its Action property. Drag and drop the Zapier Webhook action and configure the action as follows: Set the Variable property to Result. Set the Webhook URL property to the URL of your Webhook copied from your Zap. The Request data contains a data item called name that refers to the variable Your name. Thus, the Zapier Webhook will receive the parameter of the user's name entered in the Zyllio screen. Then click on the Zapier! button to trigger your Webhook. Testing the Webhook Return to the Zap, cl"}]}];
